        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_788df0e6a1c94c67a0f5457bf364876d",
      "statement": "When a removal or revocation actually removes or downgrades someone, the overseer tears down their observer state — best-effort removeObserver on all gatekeeper facets then record deletion — before the session-restarting DO abort, so stale observer state only ever errs toward stricter future checks and never a leak.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://packages/workshop-backend/src/overseer.ts#L10423-L10441",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:5b57fd83f04655a006b80ef36add0728012d54ab22b4d7eaabb484c6dc1d8287:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_13a6737bfb71444c877dd603d3aa47b8",
      "statement": "docs/observers.md §9 records the per-resource strategy table (documented strategy rather than verified per-gatekeeper behavior): A private-only (Gmail, ZoomInfo), B single-unit ACL (GitHub repo, Google Doc/Sheet/Calendar, Notion page, Supabase project, Linear team), C data-set tracking (BigQuery datasets, Supabase org, Linear/Notion workspace, Confluence, Context Library), D low-stakes no-ops (Email, Spotify, Home Assistant), and N/A (Cloudflare, auth-only); the broad-binding lens says C requires BOTH distinct per-sub-resource ACLs and a per-observer access oracle.",
